---
layout: article
title: Passkeys
description: Let users sign up and sign in with a passkey, a key pair stored on their device and unlocked with Face ID, Touch ID, a fingerprint, or a device PIN, on the web, Android, and Apple platforms.
---

A passkey replaces a password with a key pair that lives on the user's device or in their password manager, such as iCloud Keychain or Google Password Manager. The user unlocks it with Face ID, Touch ID, a fingerprint, or the device PIN. The private key never leaves the device, and the public key that Appwrite stores is useless to an attacker. A passkey only works on the domain it was created for, so a look-alike phishing site cannot use it.

Appwrite implements the [WebAuthn](https://www.w3.org/TR/webauthn-3/) standard behind passkeys. Your app passes the options Appwrite returns to the platform's passkey API, such as `navigator.credentials` in the browser or Credential Manager on Android, and sends the result back to Appwrite. The same passkey works on your website and in your Android and iOS apps, because they all share one relying party.

Sign-in is usernameless. The user picks a passkey, and Appwrite finds the account it belongs to, so your sign-in screen needs no email field.

{% info title="Passkeys and MFA" %}
Every passkey ceremony requires user verification, such as a biometric or the device PIN. A session created with a passkey already proves two factors, so it also satisfies [multi-factor authentication](/docs/products/auth/mfa).
{% /info %}

# How it works {% #how-it-works %}

Passkeys are bound to a **relying party ID**: the domain of your app, such as `example.com`. The browser or the operating system only offers a passkey on that domain and the apps that the domain trusts.

Appwrite works out where each passkey may be used from your project's platforms. You do not maintain a separate list of origins.

| Platform | What you add in Appwrite | Origin Appwrite accepts | What else the platform needs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Web | A web platform on the relying party ID or one of its subdomains | `https://app.example.com` | Nothing |
| Android | An Android platform with the SHA-256 fingerprints of your signing keys | `android:apk-key-hash:<hash>` | `/.well-known/assetlinks.json` on the relying party ID |
| Apple | An Apple platform | `https://<relying party ID>` | An associated domain and `/.well-known/apple-app-site-association` on the relying party ID |
| Local development | A `localhost` web platform | `http://localhost` on any port | Nothing |

A native app needs a domain even if you have no website. The domain only has to serve the two `.well-known` files over HTTPS.

# Turn on passkeys {% #enable %}

{% only_dark %}
![The Enable passkeys dialog suggesting a domain from the project's web platforms](/images/docs/auth/passkeys/dark/enable-passkeys.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![The Enable passkeys dialog suggesting a domain from the project's web platforms](/images/docs/auth/passkeys/enable-passkeys.avif)
{% /only_light %}

1. In the Appwrite Console, open your project and go to **Auth**, then **Settings**.
2. Turn on **Passkey**.
3. Choose the domain passkeys belong to. The dialog suggests domains from your web platforms, or you can type another one.
4. Select **Enable**.

Use the registrable domain, such as `example.com`, rather than a subdomain like `app.example.com`. A passkey created on `example.com` also works on every subdomain that is a web platform, so you can add subdomains later without asking users to create new passkeys.

{% info title="The relying party ID is permanent" %}
Every passkey is bound to the relying party ID it was created for. Once users have registered passkeys, Appwrite refuses to change it, because their passkeys would stop working.
{% /info %}

To review the setup, go to **Auth** > **Policies** > **Passkeys**. The **Where passkeys work** list shows every origin Appwrite accepts, computed from your platforms.

{% only_dark %}
![The relying party card listing where passkeys work](/images/docs/auth/passkeys/dark/relying-party.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![The relying party card listing where passkeys work](/images/docs/auth/passkeys/relying-party.avif)
{% /only_light %}

You can also configure passkeys with a server SDK and an API key with the `policies.write` and `project.write` scopes.

```server-nodejs
const sdk = require('node-appwrite');

const client = new sdk.Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // Your API Endpoint
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')                            // Your project ID
    .setKey('<API_KEY>');                                  // Your secret API key

const project = new sdk.Project(client);

await project.updatePasskeyPolicy({ rpId: 'example.com' });
await project.updateAuthMethod({ methodId: 'passkey', enabled: true });
```

# Local development {% #local-development %}

{% only_dark %}
![An app on http://localhost:5173 signed in with a passkey against Appwrite Cloud](/images/docs/auth/passkeys/dark/local-development.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![An app on http://localhost:5173 signed in with a passkey against Appwrite Cloud](/images/docs/auth/passkeys/local-development.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Add `localhost` as a web platform, and passkeys work on `http://localhost` on any port. Requests from localhost get their own relying party, `localhost`, so you can develop without a domain and without touching the production relying party ID.

Passkeys created on localhost only work on localhost. They are separate from your production passkeys, but they still count as registered passkeys: once one exists, Appwrite refuses to change the relying party ID until it is deleted. Set your production relying party ID before you test on localhost, and use `localhost`, not `127.0.0.1`.

# Set up your platforms {% #platforms %}

## Web {% #web %}

Add a web platform for each hostname your app runs on, such as `example.com` and `app.example.com`. The hostname must be the relying party ID or one of its subdomains. A wildcard platform such as `*.example.com` covers every subdomain, but not `example.com` itself.

Browsers need no other setup. Passkeys work on HTTPS origins on the default port, and on `http://localhost` on any port for development.

The web examples below use `PublicKeyCredential.parseCreationOptionsFromJSON()`, `parseRequestOptionsFromJSON()` and `toJSON()`, which need Chrome and Edge 129, Firefox 119, or Safari 18.4 and later. For older browsers, convert the options with a library such as [`@simplewebauthn/browser`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@simplewebauthn/browser).

## Android {% #android %}

{% only_dark %}
![The Update app drawer with signing certificate fingerprints for an Android app](/images/docs/auth/passkeys/dark/android-fingerprints.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![The Update app drawer with signing certificate fingerprints for an Android app](/images/docs/auth/passkeys/android-fingerprints.avif)
{% /only_light %}

An Android app does not sign in from a web origin. Android identifies it by a hash of the certificate the app is signed with, so Appwrite needs that certificate's fingerprint.

1. In the Appwrite Console, go to **Apps** and select your Android app. Add one if you have not yet.
2. Under **Signing certificate fingerprints**, paste the SHA-256 fingerprint of each key that signs your app, one per line.
3. Select **Update**.

Most apps have more than one signing key:

- **Debug builds** are signed with the debug keystore on each developer's machine. Run `./gradlew signingReport` in your Android project, which prints the SHA-256 fingerprint for every build variant.
- **Builds from Google Play** are re-signed by Google with the app signing key. In Play Console, open **App integrity**, select **Play app signing**, and copy the SHA-256 fingerprint of the **App signing key certificate**.
- **Builds you install yourself** carry the key that signed them, such as your upload key.

Android also checks that your domain trusts the app before it creates or uses a passkey. Host this file at `https://example.com/.well-known/assetlinks.json`, on your relying party ID, with your package name and the same fingerprints:

```json
[
    {
        "relation": [
            "delegate_permission/common.handle_all_urls",
            "delegate_permission/common.get_login_creds"
        ],
        "target": {
            "namespace": "android_app",
            "package_name": "com.example.app",
            "sha256_cert_fingerprints": [
                "14:6D:E9:83:C5:73:06:50:D8:EE:B9:95:2F:34:FC:64:16:A0:83:42:E6:1D:BE:A8:8A:04:96:B2:3F:CF:44:E5"
            ]
        }
    }
]
```

Add the [Credential Manager](https://developer.android.com/identity/sign-in/credential-manager) library to your app, which shows the system passkey sheet:

```kotlin
dependencies {
    implementation("androidx.credentials:credentials:1.6.0")
    implementation("androidx.credentials:credentials-play-services-auth:1.6.0")
    implementation("com.google.code.gson:gson:2.14.0")
}
```

The examples below use Gson to convert between the JSON strings Credential Manager works with and the maps the Appwrite SDK takes.

## Apple {% #apple %}

On iOS and macOS, the operating system confirms that your app may use your domain's passkeys, and the app signs in from the relying party ID itself. Appwrite accepts `https://example.com` once the project has an Apple platform.

1. In the Appwrite Console, add an Apple platform with your app's bundle ID.
2. In Xcode, open your target's **Signing & Capabilities**, add **Associated Domains**, and add `webcredentials:example.com`.
3. Host this file at `https://example.com/.well-known/apple-app-site-association`, with your team ID and bundle ID:

```json
{
    "webcredentials": {
        "apps": ["ABCDE12345.com.example.app"]
    }
}
```

Apple fetches the file through its CDN, so changes can take a while to reach devices. During development, add `?mode=developer` to the entitlement, such as `webcredentials:example.com?mode=developer`, to fetch it directly from your server.

# Register a passkey {% #register %}

{% only_dark %}
![An Android app creating an account, then saving a passkey for it](/images/docs/auth/passkeys/dark/android-register.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![An Android app creating an account, then saving a passkey for it](/images/docs/auth/passkeys/android-register.avif)
{% /only_light %}

A passkey is added to an account the user is already signed in to. For a new user, create the account and a session first, then register the passkey. For an existing user, offer to add a passkey after they sign in.

Registration takes three steps:

1. Call `createPasskey()`. Appwrite returns a `passkeyId` and the `publicKey` creation options.
2. Pass the options to the platform's passkey API. The user confirms with a biometric or the device PIN.
3. Send the resulting credential to `updatePasskeyVerification()`. Appwrite verifies it and stores the passkey.

The session must have signed in, or completed an MFA challenge, within the last 10 minutes, and step 3 must use the same session as step 1. The challenge expires after 5 minutes, and each user can have up to 10 passkeys, counting registrations still in progress.

{% tabs %}
{% tabsitem #register-web title="Web" %}
```client-web
import { Client, Account } from "appwrite";

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // Your API Endpoint
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');                           // Your project ID

const account = new Account(client);

const challenge = await account.createPasskey({ name: 'MacBook' });

const credential = await navigator.credentials.create({
    publicKey: PublicKeyCredential.parseCreationOptionsFromJSON(challenge.publicKey),
});

const passkey = await account.updatePasskeyVerification({
    passkeyId: challenge.passkeyId,
    credential: credential.toJSON(),
});
```
{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #register-flutter title="Flutter" %}
Flutter apps call the platform's passkey API through the [`passkeys`](https://pub.dev/packages/passkeys) package, which reads and returns the same JSON Appwrite uses.

{% only_dark %}
![A Flutter app listing the passkeys on the signed-in account](/images/docs/auth/passkeys/dark/flutter-passkeys.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![A Flutter app listing the passkeys on the signed-in account](/images/docs/auth/passkeys/flutter-passkeys.avif)
{% /only_light %}

```client-flutter
import 'package:appwrite/appwrite.dart';
import 'package:passkeys/authenticator.dart';
import 'package:passkeys/types.dart';

final client = Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // Your API Endpoint
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');                           // Your project ID

final account = Account(client);
final authenticator = PasskeyAuthenticator();

final challenge = await account.createPasskey(name: 'Pixel');

final response = await authenticator.register(
    RegisterRequestType.fromJson(challenge.publicKey),
);

final passkey = await account.updatePasskeyVerification(
    passkeyId: challenge.passkeyId,
    credential: response.toJson(),
);
```
{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #register-android title="Android" %}
```client-android-kotlin
import androidx.credentials.CreatePublicKeyCredentialRequest
import androidx.credentials.CreatePublicKeyCredentialResponse
import androidx.credentials.CredentialManager
import com.google.gson.Gson
import com.google.gson.reflect.TypeToken
import io.appwrite.Client
import io.appwrite.services.Account

val client = Client(context)
    .setEndpoint("https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1") // Your API Endpoint
    .setProject("<PROJECT_ID>")                            // Your project ID

val account = Account(client)
val credentialManager = CredentialManager.create(context)
val gson = Gson()
val mapType = object : TypeToken<Map<String, Any?>>() {}.type

val challenge = account.createPasskey(name = "Pixel")

val response = credentialManager.createCredential(
    activity,
    CreatePublicKeyCredentialRequest(gson.toJson(challenge.publicKey)),
) as CreatePublicKeyCredentialResponse

val passkey = account.updatePasskeyVerification(
    passkeyId = challenge.passkeyId,
    credential = gson.fromJson(response.registrationResponseJson, mapType),
)
```
{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #register-apple title="Apple" %}
```client-apple
import AuthenticationServices
import Appwrite

let client = Client()
    .setEndpoint("https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1") // Your API Endpoint
    .setProject("<PROJECT_ID>")                            // Your project ID

let account = Account(client)

let challenge = try await account.createPasskey(name: "iPhone")
let options = challenge.publicKey

let provider = ASAuthorizationPlatformPublicKeyCredentialProvider(
    relyingPartyIdentifier: (options["rp"]?.value as! [String: Any])["id"] as! String
)
let request = provider.createCredentialRegistrationRequest(
    challenge: Data(base64URLEncoded: options["challenge"]?.value as! String)!,
    name: (options["user"]?.value as! [String: Any])["name"] as! String,
    userID: Data(base64URLEncoded: (options["user"]?.value as! [String: Any])["id"] as! String)!
)

// Present an ASAuthorizationController with the request.
// In authorizationController(controller:didCompleteWithAuthorization:):
guard let registration = authorization.credential
        as? ASAuthorizationPlatformPublicKeyCredentialRegistration else { return }

let passkey = try await account.updatePasskeyVerification(
    passkeyId: challenge.passkeyId,
    credential: [
        "id": registration.credentialID.base64URLEncodedString(),
        "rawId": registration.credentialID.base64URLEncodedString(),
        "type": "public-key",
        "response": [
            "clientDataJSON": registration.rawClientDataJSON.base64URLEncodedString(),
            "attestationObject": registration.rawAttestationObject!.base64URLEncodedString(),
        ],
    ]
)
```

Foundation has no base64url helpers, so add this extension on `Data`. Appwrite expects base64url without padding.

```swift
extension Data {
    init?(base64URLEncoded string: String) {
        var base64 = string.replacingOccurrences(of: "-", with: "+").replacingOccurrences(of: "_", with: "/")
        base64 += String(repeating: "=", count: (4 - base64.count % 4) % 4)
        self.init(base64Encoded: base64)
    }

    func base64URLEncodedString() -> String {
        base64EncodedString()
            .replacingOccurrences(of: "+", with: "-")
            .replacingOccurrences(of: "/", with: "_")
            .replacingOccurrences(of: "=", with: "")
    }
}
```
{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #register-react-native title="React Native" %}
React Native apps call the platform's passkey API through the [`react-native-passkey`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/react-native-passkey) library, which reads and returns the same JSON Appwrite uses.

```client-react-native
import { Client, Account } from 'react-native-appwrite';
import { Passkey } from 'react-native-passkey';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // Your API Endpoint
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');                           // Your project ID

const account = new Account(client);

const challenge = await account.createPasskey({ name: 'iPhone' });

const credential = await Passkey.create(challenge.publicKey);

const passkey = await account.updatePasskeyVerification({
    passkeyId: challenge.passkeyId,
    credential,
});
```
{% /tabsitem %}
{% /tabs %}

# Sign in with a passkey {% #sign-in %}

{% only_dark %}
![An Android app signing in with a saved passkey from the system sheet](/images/docs/auth/passkeys/dark/android-sign-in.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![An Android app signing in with a saved passkey from the system sheet](/images/docs/auth/passkeys/android-sign-in.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Sign-in needs no email or password. The user picks one of their passkeys for your domain, and Appwrite finds the account it belongs to.

1. Call `createPasskeyToken()`. Appwrite returns a challenge with its `$id` and the `publicKey` request options.
2. Pass the options to the platform's passkey API. The user picks a passkey and confirms.
3. Send the credential to `updatePasskeyToken()`. Appwrite verifies it and returns a token with a `userId` and `secret`.
4. Exchange the token for a session with `createSession()`, as with any [token](/docs/products/auth/tokens).

The token expires after one minute, and each challenge can only be used once. Each IP address can start and complete 10 passkey sign-ins per hour.

{% tabs %}
{% tabsitem #sign-in-web title="Web" %}
```client-web
import { Client, Account } from "appwrite";

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // Your API Endpoint
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');                           // Your project ID

const account = new Account(client);

const challenge = await account.createPasskeyToken();

const credential = await navigator.credentials.get({
    publicKey: PublicKeyCredential.parseRequestOptionsFromJSON(challenge.publicKey),
});

const token = await account.updatePasskeyToken({
    challengeId: challenge.$id,
    credential: credential.toJSON(),
});

const session = await account.createSession({
    userId: token.userId,
    secret: token.secret,
});
```
{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #sign-in-flutter title="Flutter" %}
```client-flutter
import 'package:appwrite/appwrite.dart';
import 'package:passkeys/authenticator.dart';
import 'package:passkeys/types.dart';

final client = Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // Your API Endpoint
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');                           // Your project ID

final account = Account(client);
final authenticator = PasskeyAuthenticator();

final challenge = await account.createPasskeyToken();

final response = await authenticator.authenticate(
    AuthenticateRequestType.fromJson(challenge.publicKey),
);

final token = await account.updatePasskeyToken(
    challengeId: challenge.$id,
    credential: response.toJson(),
);

final session = await account.createSession(
    userId: token.userId,
    secret: token.secret,
);
```
{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #sign-in-android title="Android" %}
```client-android-kotlin
import androidx.credentials.CredentialManager
import androidx.credentials.GetCredentialRequest
import androidx.credentials.GetPublicKeyCredentialOption
import androidx.credentials.PublicKeyCredential
import com.google.gson.Gson
import com.google.gson.reflect.TypeToken
import io.appwrite.Client
import io.appwrite.services.Account

val client = Client(context)
    .setEndpoint("https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1") // Your API Endpoint
    .setProject("<PROJECT_ID>")                            // Your project ID

val account = Account(client)
val credentialManager = CredentialManager.create(context)
val gson = Gson()
val mapType = object : TypeToken<Map<String, Any?>>() {}.type

val challenge = account.createPasskeyToken()

val result = credentialManager.getCredential(
    activity,
    GetCredentialRequest(listOf(GetPublicKeyCredentialOption(gson.toJson(challenge.publicKey)))),
)
val credential = (result.credential as PublicKeyCredential).authenticationResponseJson

val token = account.updatePasskeyToken(
    challengeId = challenge.id,
    credential = gson.fromJson(credential, mapType),
)

val session = account.createSession(
    userId = token.userId,
    secret = token.secret,
)
```
{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #sign-in-apple title="Apple" %}
```client-apple
import AuthenticationServices
import Appwrite

let client = Client()
    .setEndpoint("https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1") // Your API Endpoint
    .setProject("<PROJECT_ID>")                            // Your project ID

let account = Account(client)

let challenge = try await account.createPasskeyToken()
let options = challenge.publicKey

let provider = ASAuthorizationPlatformPublicKeyCredentialProvider(
    relyingPartyIdentifier: options["rpId"]?.value as! String
)
let request = provider.createCredentialAssertionRequest(
    challenge: Data(base64URLEncoded: options["challenge"]?.value as! String)!
)

// Present an ASAuthorizationController with the request.
// In authorizationController(controller:didCompleteWithAuthorization:):
guard let assertion = authorization.credential
        as? ASAuthorizationPlatformPublicKeyCredentialAssertion else { return }

let token = try await account.updatePasskeyToken(
    challengeId: challenge.id,
    credential: [
        "id": assertion.credentialID.base64URLEncodedString(),
        "rawId": assertion.credentialID.base64URLEncodedString(),
        "type": "public-key",
        "response": [
            "clientDataJSON": assertion.rawClientDataJSON.base64URLEncodedString(),
            "authenticatorData": assertion.rawAuthenticatorData.base64URLEncodedString(),
            "signature": assertion.signature.base64URLEncodedString(),
            "userHandle": assertion.userID.base64URLEncodedString(),
        ],
    ]
)

let session = try await account.createSession(
    userId: token.userId,
    secret: token.secret
)
```
{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #sign-in-react-native title="React Native" %}
```client-react-native
import { Client, Account } from 'react-native-appwrite';
import { Passkey } from 'react-native-passkey';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // Your API Endpoint
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');                           // Your project ID

const account = new Account(client);

const challenge = await account.createPasskeyToken();

const credential = await Passkey.get(challenge.publicKey);

const token = await account.updatePasskeyToken({
    challengeId: challenge.$id,
    credential,
});

const session = await account.createSession({
    userId: token.userId,
    secret: token.secret,
});
```
{% /tabsitem %}
