# Manage passkeys {% #manage %}

{% only_dark %}
![A security settings page listing two passkeys with when each was added and last used](/images/docs/auth/passkeys/dark/manage-passkeys.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![A security settings page listing two passkeys with when each was added and last used](/images/docs/auth/passkeys/manage-passkeys.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Signed-in users can list, rename, and delete their own passkeys, for example from a security settings screen. Each passkey records its name and when it was last used. Like registration, deleting a passkey needs a session that signed in, or completed MFA, within the last 10 minutes.

{% multicode %}
```client-web
import { Client, Account } from "appwrite";

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // Your API Endpoint
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');                           // Your project ID

const account = new Account(client);

const { passkeys } = await account.listPasskeys();

await account.updatePasskey({ passkeyId: passkeys[0].$id, name: 'Work laptop' });

await account.deletePasskey({ passkeyId: passkeys[0].$id });
```
```client-flutter
import 'package:appwrite/appwrite.dart';

final client = Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // Your API Endpoint
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');                           // Your project ID

final account = Account(client);

final list = await account.listPasskeys();

await account.updatePasskey(passkeyId: list.passkeys.first.$id, name: 'Work laptop');

await account.deletePasskey(passkeyId: list.passkeys.first.$id);
```
```client-android-kotlin
import io.appwrite.Client
import io.appwrite.services.Account

val client = Client(context)
    .setEndpoint("https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1") // Your API Endpoint
    .setProject("<PROJECT_ID>")                            // Your project ID

val account = Account(client)

val list = account.listPasskeys()

account.updatePasskey(passkeyId = list.passkeys.first().id, name = "Work laptop")

account.deletePasskey(passkeyId = list.passkeys.first().id)
```
```client-apple
import Appwrite

let client = Client()
    .setEndpoint("https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1") // Your API Endpoint
    .setProject("<PROJECT_ID>")                            // Your project ID

let account = Account(client)

let list = try await account.listPasskeys()

_ = try await account.updatePasskey(passkeyId: list.passkeys[0].id, name: "Work laptop")

_ = try await account.deletePasskey(passkeyId: list.passkeys[0].id)
```
```client-react-native
import { Client, Account } from 'react-native-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // Your API Endpoint
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');                           // Your project ID

const account = new Account(client);

const { passkeys } = await account.listPasskeys();

await account.updatePasskey({ passkeyId: passkeys[0].$id, name: 'Work phone' });

await account.deletePasskey({ passkeyId: passkeys[0].$id });
```
{% /multicode %}

On the server, the Users service has the same operations for any user: `users.listPasskeys()`, `users.getPasskey()`, `users.updatePasskey()` and `users.deletePasskey()`. Use them to remove a lost device's passkey on a user's behalf. Deleting a user also deletes their passkeys.

# Errors {% #errors %}

| Error | Cause |
| --- | --- |
| `user_auth_method_unsupported` | Passkeys are turned off, or the project has no relying party ID with a matching platform. |
| `user_reauthentication_required` | Registering or deleting a passkey needs a session that signed in, or completed MFA, within the last 10 minutes. |
| `user_invalid_token` | The challenge expired after 5 minutes or was already used, registration finished from a different session, the passkey settings changed during the ceremony, or the sign-in token expired after one minute. Start again. |
| `user_passkey_invalid` | The credential failed verification, for example because the origin is not one Appwrite accepts or the signature does not match. |
| `user_passkey_limit_exceeded` | The user already has 10 passkeys, counting registrations still in progress. |
| `user_passkey_already_exists` | This credential, or a passkey with this ID, is already registered. |
| `user_passkey_not_found` | No passkey with this ID belongs to the user, or the registration is no longer pending. |
| `user_blocked` | The passkey belongs to a blocked user. |
| `general_argument_invalid` | You tried to change the relying party ID while users have passkeys, including localhost passkeys and registrations in progress. |
| `general_rate_limit_exceeded` | Too many passkey requests. Registration allows 10 per user and sign-in 10 per IP address each hour. |

When registration or sign-in fails with `user_passkey_invalid` from a native app, compare the origin the platform sent with **Where passkeys work** under **Auth** > **Policies** > **Passkeys**. On Android, a missing origin usually means the app was signed with a key whose fingerprint is not on the Android platform.
